course: dictionary
lesson: 5499
title: Lawmaker: the people who make the laws
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A lawmaker is a person who helps make laws. Look at the two parts of the word: law and maker. A lawmaker is a law maker. «In a democracy the people choose their leaders and lawmakers by voting.» The sentence tells you lawmakers are chosen by voters, and they are the people who make laws.
explain: «Power is split into three parts: lawmakers make the laws, the government carries them out, and the courts decide whether a law has been broken.» Here the sentence gives you the job itself. Lawmakers make the laws, while the government and the courts do different jobs. Check your meaning by swapping it in: "people who make laws" fits every time.
explain: Lawmakers have different names in different places. In the USA they sit in Congress, and in the UK and Australia they sit in Parliament. Whatever the place, the job is the same: they debate ideas, vote on new laws and change old ones.
fact: A lawmaker is a person who helps make laws.
fact: Lawmaker is made from two words: law and maker.
fact: Lawmakers are often elected, which means voters choose them.
fact: Lawmakers can sit in parliament or congress, depending on the country.
fact: Lawmakers debate ideas, vote on new laws and change old ones.
exercise: What is a lawmaker? | *A person who helps make laws | A police officer who stops crime | A person who writes books about law | A person who decides whether a law has been broken
exercise: In «Power is split into three parts: lawmakers make the laws, the government carries them out, and the courts decide whether a law has been broken», which part makes the laws? | The government | The police | The courts | *The lawmakers
exercise: What word in «In a democracy the people choose their leaders and lawmakers by voting» shows how lawmakers get their jobs? | democracy | leaders | people | *voting
test: Why is power shared between lawmakers, the government and the courts? | So that the courts can make new laws | So that nobody needs to vote | *So that each one can check the others | So that the lawmakers can rule alone
test: In the USA, where do lawmakers sit? | The supermarket | *Congress | The school playground | The hospital
exam: Which sentence uses lawmaker correctly? | *Elected lawmakers debate ideas and vote on new laws | Lawmakers are the people who cook the food in a restaurant | Lawmakers are the bright lights that shine in the night sky | The lawmaker swam across the river to the island
